      "kind": "interpretive_questions",
      "title": "Sin as an active power",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_3",
          "text": "Romans 7 uses active language for sin: it seizes opportunity, deceives, kills, inhabits, wages war, and takes captive within the disputed first-person discourse. This language should not be collapsed into a simple account of physical death or used as a psychiatric diagnosis; the passage's personification and its claims about human agency need to remain distinct.",
          "verse_refs": [
            "Romans 7:8-23"
